  "Modify or Extend Existing RTL": "Fix interface-level mismatches first: add every testbench-visible control/status port before tuning behavior.\nFor scheduler extensions, preserve baseline add/cancel/priority flow and layer modify/recurring/logging with minimal extra state.\nKeep error sticky across idle cycles and clear it only on successful control operations to satisfy delayed assertions.\nWhen recurring events trigger, reschedule by interval instead of invalidating the slot.\nUse temporary arrays for atomic per-cycle updates, then commit at cycle end to avoid ordering bugs.",
  "Integrate multiple modules into a top module": "Fix compile/elaboration blockers first by creating the exact expected top module/file names before functional tuning.\nFor async unlock/control signals crossing into pclk logic, use a 2-flop synchronizer in the top-level wrapper.\nKeep integration changes minimal: preserve submodule interfaces and route existing outputs through the new top-level unchanged.\nImplement security gating at the controlled function boundary (history update path) so APB CSR behavior stays backward compatible.\nWhen unlock sequences are pulse-driven, make the FSM resilient to idle cycles and reset only on incorrect write attempts.",
  "Create new RTL from spec": "Create the exact expected top module/file first to clear elaboration blockers before functional tuning.\nImplement explicit width selection (8/16/32/64) via `sel` and keep masked/zero-extended payload handling deterministic.\nDrive `done` as a clean transaction-complete pulse and keep reset behavior unambiguous across TX/RX state.\nUse a simple, test-stable TX/RX handshake path, then layer Gray conversion as `data ^ (data >> 1)` on reconstructed output.",
  "Debug/fix buggy RTL": "Start from the first failing assertion and inspect staged RTL operators first; data-transform bugs often compile but break functional checks.\nIn true dual-port RAM fixes, keep memory width exactly DATA_WIDTH and remove unintended write/read data mangling (XOR/shift/add/sub).\nUse a single posedge sequential process for both ports and keep deterministic same-address write precedence via assignment order."
}


